Add a VP8 backend and advertise it

VP8 decodes bit-exactly on this hardware -- six vectors covering all four
profiles, i.e. every reconstruction and loop-filter pair the engine implements
-- but only through GStreamer's v4l2slvp8dec, straight to the kernel.  Nothing
could reach it through VA-API, because this driver had no VP8 backend and
neither does upstream: master carries h264.c, h265.c and mpeg2.c and nothing
else, on one branch, and none of its pull requests add one.  So this is new
code rather than a port, written against the V4L2 uAPI and the VP8 spec.

Three things in the mapping are not obvious, and all three are places where the
naive reading is wrong.

THE UNCOMPRESSED DATA CHUNK HAS TO BE PUT BACK.  ffmpeg strips it before
handing over the slice data:

	unsigned int header_size = 3 + 7 * s->keyframe;
	const uint8_t *data = buffer + header_size;

while cedrus expects to find it -- it starts the engine at header_size * 8
(VE_H264_VLD_OFFSET) and locates the partition size table at
first_part_size + header_size (VE_VP8_PART_SIZE_OFFSET).  Without it every
offset past the frame tag is wrong.  The shim therefore synthesises the chunk
ahead of the slice data.  Only its LENGTH is load-bearing, since the engine is
pointed past the bytes, but it is filled in correctly anyway: it costs nothing,
it keeps a dumped OUTPUT buffer a parseable VP8 frame, and it avoids silently
depending on the engine continuing to skip it.

This is safe to do when the slice data arrives because ffmpeg renders every
parameter buffer before any slice buffer, and the slice parameters before the
slice data, so the frame type and first partition size are both known by then.

FIRST_PART_SIZE IS RECOVERED BY INVERSION, NOT MEASUREMENT.  VA-API does not
send it.  ffmpeg sends what is LEFT of the control partition after it parsed
the header itself:

	sp.partition_size[0] = s->header_partition_size -
			       ((sp.macroblock_offset + 7) / 8);

so adding that term back gives the true size exactly.  Deriving it from
slice_data_size and the token partition sizes -- the obvious alternative --
is wrong the moment a frame has more than one token partition.

SEGMENT VALUES ARE ABSOLUTE, QUANTISER DELTAS ARE NOT.  VA-API reports the
per-segment quantiser and loop-filter values already adjusted, so
V4L2_VP8_SEGMENT_FLAG_DELTA_VALUE_MODE is left CLEAR and they pass through
unchanged; cedrus reads that as VE_VP8_PPS_MB_SEGMENT_ABS_DELTA, which is the
same meaning.  The per-plane quantiser deltas are the opposite case: V4L2 wants
deltas, VA-API sends six absolute indices per segment, so they are recovered by
subtraction against segment 0.  That stays exact even where ffmpeg's clamp to
[0,127] bit, because it emits clip(base + delta) and the engine recomputes
clip(base + delta') with delta' = clip(base + delta) - base, which is the same
number; delta' fits the signed field.

One inversion worth naming: VA-API's key_frame field means the OPPOSITE of its
name -- "0 means a key frame", and ffmpeg fills it as !s->keyframe -- while
V4L2_VP8_FRAME_FLAG_KEY_FRAME is set when the frame is one.

VA-API also sends the coefficient probabilities in a buffer of their own,
which no other codec here uses, so VAProbabilityBufferType is handled for the
first time -- in RequestCreateBuffer as well as when storing it.  Accepting it
in only one of those places fails at the client with "the requested
VABufferType is not supported" long before any of the mapping above runs.

The profile is added to BOTH RequestQueryConfigProfiles and
RequestQueryConfigEntrypoints, gated as ever on the kernel really offering
V4L2_PIX_FMT_VP8_FRAME.  A profile listed in only one of the two is invisible
in exactly the way an unported codec is -- vainfo drops it and vaCreateConfig
has nothing to match -- which is the trap patch 0011 documented.


Adding a tenth profile also ran into a latent bug: RequestQueryConfigProfiles
bounded itself with V4L2_REQUEST_MAX_CONFIG_ATTRIBUTES (10) rather than
V4L2_REQUEST_MAX_PROFILES (11), which is the constant the driver actually
reports to libva as max_profiles.  With nine profiles already listed the guard
was false and VP8 was dropped on the floor -- advertised nowhere, with no
error, which is a third way to be invisible on top of the two above.  The four
guards now use the profile constant.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
